WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:03.300
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.660
In this video I want to talk about A Quiet
Place and some intriguing deleted scenes and

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.429
rejected concepts, plus how the creature design
changed over the course of production.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:16.309
If you're finding me for the first time, I
do lots of movie deleted scenes and breakdowns,

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.070
so do subscribe if you enjoy this.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.900
Obviously, spoilers ahead for the movie, so
take care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.920
The movie's producers revealed that the original
script included flashbacks.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.090
Though the producers weren't specific in their
Hollywood Reporter interview,In fact,initially,

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:33.180
the loss of the family's youngest son was
going to be "handled in a flashback".

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.799
However, when director and star John Krasinski
did his rewrite of Bryan Woods and Scott Beck's

00:00:37.799 --> 00:00:43.720
script, he wanted to make that shocking moment
feel more urgent, so he cut the flashback,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.159
brought the scene into the present and moved
it right up to the front of the movie.

00:00:48.159 --> 00:00:52.260
Losing that flashback and shifting the scene
to the start of the film was a great call

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.760
by Krasinski as it made the terrifying reality
of the family's situation totally clear from

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.280
the get-go; basically, the unthinkable becomes
all-too-imaginable for both the family and

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.780
the audience.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.140
Other flashbacks which featured the characters
talking were also given the chop by Krasinski.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.330
Though the producers weren't specific in their
Hollywood Reporter interview, I think the

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.740
deleted dialogue may well have filled in some
of the Abbott's backstory and as well as background

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.940
about the invasion of the creatures.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.131
Speaking of backstory, in the trailers, there's
a scene which was deleted from the film, which

00:01:24.131 --> 00:01:29.100
shows Emily Blunt's character Evelyn watching
what looks like news footage on various monitors

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.690
and there are also voiceovers from different
news reports.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.010
[FEEDBACK SOUND]
"... a massive invasion ..."

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.999
"... around the world ..."
"... an attack ..."

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:40.380
"... total devastation ..."
[FEEDBACK SOUND]

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.380
This looks like it was probably part of some
early exposition about the alien invasion

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.800
which ended up being cut from the movie.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.920
What's also interesting in this deleted scene
is that this version of the father's white

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.510
board wasn't in the actual film.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.070
According to this board, there were three
creatures to start and that increased to five.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.450
But in the final film, the white board clearly
shows that there are only three confirmed

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.380
creatures in the area; and at the end of the
movie, Evelyn kills one of those and we see

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.569
two others on the monitors as they approach
the house.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:14.590
I imagine the filmmakers reduced the number
of monsters in the area as they wanted to

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.430
leave the ending more realistically hopeful
for the family, and if they'd had four creatures

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.150
left to kill at once, their odds would have
been significantly slimmer.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.890
While including more background about the
Abbotts and the invasion in the film could

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.450
have been interesting, personally, I liked
the way we were dropped into the family's

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.810
daily life in the middle of this apocalyptic
situation without getting oodles of expositionary

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.880
dialogue about the characters' or creatures'
backgrounds.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.420
While you could never accuse John Krasinski's
film of being overly wordy, the draft screenplay

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.410
for A Quiet Place only had three words of
dialogue in it.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:53.740
In that early script, the characters communicated
via gestures and hand motions, but it wasn't

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.850
until Krasinski came on board to direct, star,
and rewrite, that the idea of using American

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.540
Sign Language was included in the film and
Krasinski also added the cochlear implant

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.960
being the key to the creatures' defeat.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.870
These changes made a huge impact on the final
movie, especially since Krasinski pushed to

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.490
cast deaf actress Millicent Simmonds as his
on-screen teen daughter, Regan.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.710
Simmonds injected her real-life experience
of deafness into the film, teaching the cast

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:23.750
sign language on set and advising Krasinski
on several key moments for their characters.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.959
For instance, she told Metro that the scene
where Krasinski's character Lee argues with

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:33.080
Regan after he stops her going into the basement
was initially going to play out rather differently,

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.750
with Regan backing down to her father's authority.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:40.200
However, during filming, Simmonds suggested
her character would actually be rebellious

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.860
in that moment, and Krasinski embraced that
idea wholeheartedly.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:49.099
Also, originally, before Lee's sacrifice at
the end, he was only going to sign "I love

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:50.340
you" to his children.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:55.379
However, Simmonds proposed a subtle, but very
effective change, which was for Lee to also

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:57.489
sign "I have always loved you".

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:01.690
As Regan's brother told his father earlier
in the film, Regan felt her dad blamed her

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:06.349
for the death of her little brother and no
longer loved her, though that wasn't the case.

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:11.290
So, thanks to the additional signing, Lee's
final moments see him act on his son's earlier

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.680
suggestion to tell Regan what he feels for
her, and that really heightened the emotional

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.549
impact of Lee's fate while mending his strained
relationship with his daughter.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:24.210
All of which was a great addition to the final
film.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:28.159
The original design for the creatures was
very different to what we see in the film.

00:04:28.159 --> 00:04:32.680
According to visual effects supervisor Scott
Farrar, initially the monsters looked much

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.659
less imposing and had rhinoceros-style horns
on their faces.

00:04:36.659 --> 00:04:40.659
They were actually well into post-production
when Farrar and John Krasinski realised that

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:44.630
the creatures needed a complete redesign as
they just weren't frightening enough.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.409
In fact, when they placed them in the movie,
they looked a little silly.

00:04:48.409 --> 00:04:53.610
Farrar suggested taking the VFX in a totally
different direction, going for something that

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.469
looked, as he told Vanity Fair, much more
'gross...

00:04:56.469 --> 00:04:58.120
really medical and raw'.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.520
Of course, silence and sound were always at
the heart of the film, so getting the right

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.379
look for the creature's ears was crucial and
that was also altered a number of times during

00:05:07.379 --> 00:05:12.229
production, with the monster's hearing canals
being moved around the body from the chest,

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:14.610
to the head, elsewhere, and back again.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:19.800
For the final design of the aliens though,
the filmmakers looked to real life for inspiration,

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.860
including a sea creature with very poor eyesight
called the nautilus, bats, snakes, prehistoric

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.499
fish, and even bog bodies, which are human
corpses mummified in peat.

00:05:29.499 --> 00:05:34.559
Reading through your comments on my Ending
and Creatures Explained video, I noticed some

00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:38.970
of you had issues with details in the movie
that you felt created plot holes, one of which

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.189
was to do with the family's electricity supply.

00:05:41.189 --> 00:05:45.520
Now, it's interesting you brought that up
because in an interview with Slash Film, screenwriters

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.550
Scott Beck and Bryan Woods have said that,
as they were writing, they did think through

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:53.900
the logic of many different aspects of the
film's premise and have answers to a good

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.639
number of those questions.

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:59.330
Their draft script explained lots of those
details such as how the Abbotts got their

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.520
electricity through generators placed underground
so they didn't make any noise above ground.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.999
I think such details were dropped from the
final film as director and co-writer John

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:13.300
Krasinski felt they weren't crucial to the
overall movie and wanted to concentrate on

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.639
creating interesting character moments and
interactions while ratcheting up the tension

00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:21.669
throughout and still keep the run-time to
a tight 90 minutes.

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:25.199
I'm sure you can think of more than a few
movies you've seen yourself with scenes that

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:28.240
are bogged down in exposition and over-explaining
themselves.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.729
But co-writer Bryan Woods said that they really
wanted to 'slowly paint in the world these

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.300
characters are living in' by showing us a
newspaper headline' here and there or an 'awkward

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.630
interaction at the [family] dinner table'.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.460
As Woods has also said, 'I think you can make
[assumptions] and give the finished film the

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.080
benefit of the doubt.'

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:49.082
Before the movie was released, there was a
theory doing the rounds online for a while

00:06:49.082 --> 00:06:53.050
that A Quiet Place was going to be part of
the Cloverfield universe.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.710
And when the writers Beck and Woods pitched
the movie to the studio, they did initially

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.119
fear that Paramount, which also distributes
the Cloverfield movies, might try and make

00:07:01.119 --> 00:07:02.879
the film fit into that franchise.

00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:07.429
I can see the mysterious alien monsters in
the movie could potentially have been incorporated

00:07:07.429 --> 00:07:11.610
into the Cloverfield mythology, however, I'm
glad Paramount shared the writers' view that

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:15.229
A Quiet Place was strong enough to stand on
its own two feet.

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:19.770
And if there are Quiet Place sequels or anthology
films, the producers don’t intend for there

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:23.249
to be any crossover with Cloverfield in the
future either.

00:07:23.249 --> 00:07:27.650
Speaking to Cinemablend, Andrew Form and Brad
Fuller said, "That's not our world.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:30.329
[…] That's J.J.'s world and he's carefully
curating that.

00:07:30.329 --> 00:07:33.819
[…] I think John [Krasinski] has created
this amazing world with these characters and

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:36.610
I think A Quiet Place definitely lives on
its own."

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:41.199
Although the movie does, of course, lean into
horror tropes, I think it managed to create

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:44.199
something that felt fresh and was riveting
to watch.

00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:48.419
The writers have said they already have a
number of ideas and set-pieces they didn't

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:52.550
end up using in the film, so it'd be interesting
to see what they come up with for a follow-up.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:53.550
Meanwhile, in another interview with MovieWeb,
the writer further teased his sequel plans,

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:54.550
saying:
"We even have some of the set-pieces already

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:55.550
written for it.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:56.550
In a sense, that's what you have to base it
around.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:57.550
You have to figure out, what is the worst
possible situation to put your characters

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:58.550
in and go from there."

00:07:58.550 --> 00:07:59.550
On whether we can expect to see the same characters
again, he said:

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:00.550
"I think you could continue down one path
that's very obvious, or you could spin off

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:01.550
into a completely unexpected direction.

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:02.550
Which is certainly always interesting for
us.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:03.550
The uncharted territory of filmmaking is always
where you find the best inspiration."

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:04.550
Now, what do you think about the changes and
cut scenes in A Quiet Place?

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.550
What were your favourite moments in the movie
and what would you like to see happen in a

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.550
sequel?

